

findings from today were:

* Verizon AVPN path is suspicious. When the Verizon path was taken out and traffic was forced over the AT&T AVPN path, the traces and user experience looked better. They want the site to stay on AT&T only for a few days and compare behavior from that point forward.
* Open a Verizon ticket now. That is your action. The reason should be intermittent slowness/connectivity observed while traffic was using the Verizon path; after Verizon was removed, traffic was forced to AT&T for comparison and performance appeared improved.
* Wireless is a separate concern. AP31 had 30+ clients, and they want a OneSource Ekahau wireless survey to check coverage, density, interference, placement, and whether another AP is needed in the conference/training area.
* Linda’s wired issue had a local negotiation problem. Her switch port Te1/0/34 was initially negotiating at 100 Mbps. The cable diagnostic bounced the link, and when it came back it negotiated at 1 Gbps. TDR showed all four pairs normal. Her speed improved afterward. So that looks more like phone/NIC/endpoint negotiation than a bad cable.
* The link-down/up log around 16:45 GMT corresponds to the cable diagnostic they intentionally ran, so that flap itself is not evidence of an intermittent physical fault.
* The large output-drop totals and unknown-protocol-drop totals are historical counters. Nothing in what they showed proves those were actively driving today’s issue.
* The CNJ routing/BGP observation is another separate lead. Abhishek found a BGP neighbor down and route/prefix differences on the CSR/R01/R03 side, but Eric specifically said that needs to go to Tony/Mario because it’s deeper NYL/DC routing and may also relate to the existing CNJ fiber-cut incident.


